00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:06.389 align:start position:0%
 
so a little off topic here  but micah sometimes i go back and look  at old interviews when i'm prepping for  another one and our first one was for  your first feature  at any price and i know first features  can make a big impression on someone so  is there anything about your experience  working with ramin on that that you  still  find yourself thinking back to today oh  man absolutely i i feel like i learned  everything on that set  um it it changed  the course of my life you know right  right prior to to booking that job i was  kiteboarding professionally and um i was  on a completely different path  and i think if i didn't have such an  incredible experience  on that set um  i really don't know if i would have  continued to do it um  and what was so cool about that project  it was ramin's  first kind of nothing was a big movie  but but a bigger movie he had done some  shorts and smaller films  and so it's kind of both of us with like  zac efron and dennis quaid who have done  tons of movies because we're kind of  these like newbies being like  this is crazy so it's a really um  special  first experience that does not surprise  me at all he was  one of my first film professors so i  just hold him in the highest regard  because i don't think  so i know veena is a super collaborative  director  so for both of you here was there any  particular scene that you needed to  workshop with her  more than any other um i think within  all of the scenes  um it was just about having an open  dialogue about  what direction we were going to go in  and just like how far we could take it  with carl especially because i feel like  she gave me so many words and there was  like a lot  to say and chew on and it could go in a  lot of different ways so  in the rehearsal room it became  conversations about well  you know how far do you want me to go  with this do you want me to  like really just let it fly and be like  kind of over the top and lean into it or  do you want  it to be a more subtle thing and i think  ultimately what we arrived at is  we would have time to explore all the  different ways on set  but just for her to know and give me  permission to like  let it fly don't be afraid like this is  um  you know a character um  that that has a lot to chew him on  because he is a lot to chew on so don't  be afraid to go in that direction and i  can always reel you  in it was really intense i mean it was  you know for me  pretty much every scene every day um  night shoots a lot of takes of these  scenes  and so it was really nice to have um  [Music]  a person kind of like what dane was  saying like willing to really  push the boundaries and maybe we don't  use that take and maybe it's  it's not what is right for this thing  but but giving the space to be able to  like really  go there and see how that feels i love  finding out what a director's tell is  behind the monitor when you guys know  while you're doing a scene  that veena is really feeling a take so  is there anything she does that kind of  tells you like she's into this right now  i i don't know it seems like she would  get really excited i mean she's  she she wears her heart on her sleeve so  i feel like when she  um was really excited about a take you  could definitely tell  she's super into it you know i mean she  loves doing what she  she loves doing what she does and we  moved at a fairly  fast pace in making this so there was  never time to let up  you know um i'm a fairly  singularly minded person and i can't  really i certainly can't focus on like  multiple things  while i'm doing a take so i never got to  like peeper behind the monitor  when she like really was loving  something but she definitely always had  this energy of  you know moving forward and trying to  make it better and she always went in  there like with a note with an  idea ready to go on to the next take and  um i don't know that i i ever noticed  like  um a difference between when she was  into it and when she wasn't  with that singular mind frame on set  what was it like for you watching the  whole thing through for the very first  time was there anything that made you  say like holy [ __ ] that never even  registered for me on set  i mean i really loved watching it you  know i think i had a great time watching  it  um it was a lot of fun to shoot um  so i was definitely happy in viewing it  that like it's not just a thriller it's  not just  scary but um there's also it's also so  much  fun to watch you know i think there's  times that at least it makes me  laugh and i don't think i think it would  make anybody laugh and i think  you are scared but you also a lot of  times have a smile on your face and it's  really  a lot of ups and downs so i was  definitely happy that the joy that i  felt making it  um you know i i feel like the viewer has  a joy in watching it also  totally agree yeah not to like veer it  into the darkness now  but i'm always interested in hearing  about backstory and i don't know how  specific you get with your prep but did  you have to pinpoint  i guess i would say maybe the life event  that kind of  put carl on this path and got him  obsessed with the idea of playing god  with the algorithm  i didn't necessarily give him  like a life event that made him fall  into this i think it was just important  to know that like this wasn't his first  time doing it  um and he's been successful with it in  the past  and like that success has made him have  this like really god  complex where he feels like he's  invincible where he feels like he's got  it all figured out  um you know and i i don't even know that  um and i i i feel like those for those  reasons this was probably the first time  it really spiraled out of control  and he um you know he got as violent as  he gets  like i don't know that he's ever gotten  this violent in his experiments before  but i think while he's gotten this like  oh like basically i  am god i can predict people's behavior  mentality  that as it starts to slip he there's a  rage that builds up inside of him  you know and i i don't necessarily i  didn't necessarily pin point that down  to one event in his like past your life  or anything  spinning it around to end on a positive  note here you know we've been talking a  lot about  you know the negative effects of having  social media and technology in our lives  non-stop  what is one thing that both of you use  social media the internet you name it  for that i guess brightens your day  as an actor you know a fan base and  people that are coming to see your  movies is so important and so having a  way  to connect and kind of  show your i don't know that it  i don't know your normal life i don't  know it just it seems like a really kind  of cool way  to um  connect with people i guess that that  um are a fan and watching movies i mean  like i really love  looking at fan art you know um uh  it's cool and then i think it's a great  way to connect with fans  just to see that like oh i've made an  impact on their life and they're like  creating this thing and then tagging me  in it and i love looking at it for sure  um you know i also work a lot all around  the world so it's a great way  for me to keep in touch with all these  people i've worked with  um in italy and in south africa and  you know in like the arctic and like  just places  all around the world that i've worked  that i think it would be so hard to keep  in touch with them otherwise  you know i have like a driver named  cheslin that  he drove me on chronicle in cape town  south africa  like i don't know how long it was now  10-ish years ago  and um we still keep in contact and like  i still know what he's doing and like  without social media i don't think we'd  be able to do that  i love hearing that thank you guys for  hanging out for me with me  thanks great congratulations again thank  you so much

